---
/**
 * PanelHost — the ONE stacking drawer host for a page that shows more than one KIND of sheet.
 *
 * Oleh's law, 2026-08-03: **no overlapping panels anywhere · one stack, up to 10 panels · an
 * entity's drawer looks and behaves IDENTICALLY from every section.** The Data page used to mount
 * FOUR independent stacks — RecordPanel, MediaPanel, DataChangelog and EntityPanel — each with its
 * own `position: fixed; inset: 0` root at z-index 60/62/60/62, its own rail, its own ＋ and its own
 * toast. They sat on top of one another, which is the defect the founder reported.
 *
 * This is NOT a new host. It is the generalisation of the one that already existed: `MediaPanel`
 * was already a three-kind host with a discriminated panel union, a prefix-keyed `panelKey`, a
 * prefix `canOpen` and a `buildPanel(id)` that dispatched on the id prefix. This file is that
 * dispatch, lifted out so four components can share it, plus the control column `/schema` already
 * had. The kinds themselves live with the components that own their `<template>` and their body —
 * see `components/ui/panelHostRegistry.ts` for the contract.
 *
 * What the host owns: the single root · `panelsEl` · the control column (＋ · Collapse all ·
 * Close all · count · shutter · whole-background drag) · the one toast · the one `createPanelStack`
 * · Escape · `app:tabchange` · resize · and the three stack verbs in every panel header
 * (`data-ps-close` / `data-ps-detach` / `data-ps-park`), which are stack-level, not content-level.
 *
 * WHERE THE KEYBOARD CONTRACT LIVES: not here. Grepping this file for `.focus(` returns nothing and
 * always will — that is the design, not the gap the audit read it as (D45 item 1, 2026-08-14). Focus
 * entering a sheet on open, Tab/Shift+Tab cycling INSIDE it, and the keyboard going back to whatever
 * opened it on close are stack mechanics, identical for all four kinds, so they live once in
 * `panelStack.ts` (`focusPanel` / `restoreFocus` / the capture-phase Tab handler, and the `opener`
 * field on `StackPanel`). It is CONTAINMENT, never a trap: this host is non-modal by ruling — no
 * scrim, `.ph-wrap` is `pointer-events: none`, the page behind stays clickable — so clicking the
 * page is a real exit and Escape is the other. A kind that re-renders its own body wholesale has to
 * hand the keyboard back to its sheet afterwards, because that swap destroys the node focus is on;
 * `schema/entityPanelController.ts` and `data/RecordPanel.astro` each do so at their one swap.
 *
 * What a kind owns: its template, its body, its data snapshot, its own in-body reads, and its ＋
 * picker (they are genuinely different questions — "which record", "which file in the FILTERED
 * set", "which backup to compare").
 *
 * TABLEGAP is 64 on `/data` and 150 on `/schema`. That is deliberate and must not be "unified": the
 * number is the width a page reserves to the LEFT of the drawer, and the Data listings need less
 * peek than a schema tree does — Browse's left gutter (indent, chevrons, kind glyphs) is what says
 * where you are, so it has to stay visible behind the stack. It is a PROP, not a constant, for
 * exactly that reason.
 *
 * MINCONTENT is TABLEGAP's split-mode counterpart and became a prop for the same reason on
 * 2026-08-06 — it was a bare 700 in this file while the host was mounting on five routes. It is the
 * width the page CONTENT keeps once the panels take width for real, so it is a claim about a
 * particular page and every number below was measured on that page, not copied:
 *
 *   data     700  Oleh's, 2026-08-05, measured on the Browse listing. Unchanged.
 *   schema   800  A Browse row spends 480px before a single character of the entity name (twist 20 +
 *                 kind glyph 20 + Type 152 + Status 144 + Tagged 128 + chevron 16 — all fixed), and
 *                 the tree indents up to 136px more. Measured: the deepest row's name stops
 *                 truncating at 772 and disappears entirely at 687, and the tree starts scrolling
 *                 sideways below 674. 800 leaves the deepest field its full name. Data's 700 would
 *                 leave it 84px — the left gutter that carries the orientation would have eaten the
 *                 thing it orients you to.
 *   reports  720  Measured on a report definition: the rendered document (`.rptk-body-inner`) starts
 *                 overflowing its own column below 702, and its tables begin painting PAST the
 *                 content edge — i.e. underneath the panels — below 678. 720 clears both. Note it is
 *                 NOT 700: Data's number happens to land 18px under this page's own floor, which is
 *                 exactly the drift a copied constant produces.
 *
 * `section` is the other per-page fact: the arrangement is cached under `panels:<section>` so that
 * leaving Schema and coming back restores Schema's panels and not Data's. A tab switch inside a
 * section costs nothing (see the `app:tabchange` note below); the SECTION boundary is the one that
 * saves and restores.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Which section's arrangement this host caches — `panels:<section>` in sessionStorage. */
  section?: string;
  /** Overlay-mode peek reserved to the LEFT of the stack, control column included. */
  tablegap?: number;
  /** SPLIT-mode floor: the width this page's CONTENT keeps, always. See the header for each number. */
  mincontent?: number;
}
const { section = 'data', tablegap = 64, mincontent = 700 } = Astro.props;
---

{/* The three per-page numbers travel as data attributes: a component `<script>` is a separate bundle
    and cannot read `Astro.props`. Read once, in `wirePanelHost`. */}
<div class="ph-wrap" data-panelhost data-ph-section={section} data-ph-tablegap={String(tablegap)} data-ph-mincontent={String(mincontent)} hidden>
  {/* Non-modal, exactly as all four predecessors were: no scrim, the page stays clickable behind. */}
  <div class="ph-panels" data-ph-panels>
    {/* The stack CONTROL COLUMN (pattern-multi-panel-drawer) — the same column `/schema` has, wearing
        the same shared `ps-rail*` classes (styles/global.css). Stack-level verbs only; panel-level
        actions stay in each panel's header. Its whole background is the stack-resize surface; the
        shutter pill inside it is the visible affordance and the keyboard (←/→) route. ＋ is always
        visible; the two bulk verbs are revealed on hover AND :focus-within, at reserved size. */}
    <div class="ph-rail ps-rail" data-ph-rail hidden>
      <div class="ps-rail-top">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-primary btn-circle ps-rail-add tooltip tooltip-left" data-ph-add aria-label="Open another panel beside" data-tip="Open another panel beside">
          <span class="iconify lucide--plus size-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        {/* A hairline between the ＋ and the verbs below it. They are different KINDS of action —
            ＋ adds a panel, the rest act on the panels already there — and at 40px wide with no gap
            the two groups read as one column of four equal buttons. Oleh, 2026-08-06: the ＋ and the
            split toggle sit close enough that he expected to mis-click. Cheaper than moving anything. */}
        <span class="ps-rail-div" data-ph-split-div aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {/* The split-view mode toggle (components/ui/splitView.ts). A MODE, not a verb — so it is
            always visible (like ＋, unlike the two hover-revealed bulk verbs) and shows its state at
            rest through `aria-pressed`, which is both the a11y fact and the styling hook.
            The `data-tip` here is only the pre-hydration placeholder; `paintSplit()` owns it, and
            what it says is the state the press moves you TO, never the one you are already in — a
            tooltip that describes the status quo on a control whose whole job is to leave it made
            you read the button backwards. `aria-pressed` is where the current state lives. */}
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square ph-rail-mode tooltip tooltip-left" data-ph-split aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Turn on split view" data-tip="Turn on split view — panels sit beside the page">
          <span class="iconify lucide--columns-2 size-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        {/* Revealed at 3+ panels: a bulk verb that acts on one or two panels is a slower way to press
            the control already in that panel's own header. `hidden` is driven from onLayout. */}
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square ps-rail-bulk tooltip tooltip-left" data-ph-collapse-all aria-label="Collapse all panels" data-tip="Collapse all panels to strips">
          <span class="iconify lucide--chevrons-right-left size-4" data-ph-fold-ic aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost btn-square ps-rail-bulk ps-rail-closeall tooltip tooltip-left" data-ph-close-all aria-label="Close all panels" data-tip="Close all panels">
          <span class="iconify lucide--x size-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="ps-shutter tooltip tooltip-left" data-ph-shutter aria-label="Drag to resize all panels" data-tip="Drag to resize all panels equally">
        <span class="ps-shutter-pill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      {/* The count sits at the FOOT of the column, not under the buttons: it is a readout, not a
          verb, and pinning it to the bottom balances the control cluster at the top so the drag
          surface between them reads as the column's own body. */}
      <span class="ps-rail-count" data-ph-count aria-hidden="true" hidden></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{/* OUTSIDE `.ph-wrap`, not merely outside the panels row. `layout()` sets the wrap `hidden` the
    moment the stack empties — which is exactly when "Closed 3 panels · Undo" needs to be on screen,
    so a toast inside it was rendered 0x0 and the undo was unreachable. EntityPanel had already
    learned this and says so in a comment above its own toast; the merge put this one back inside.
    Sibling of the wrap, so nothing can hide it by hiding the drawer. */}
{/* The ONE ＋ picker (components/ui/panelAddPicker.ts). It used to be three near-identical popovers,
    one per kind, and the ＋ opened whichever belonged to the focused panel — so you could only add
    what you were already looking at. It opens scoped to that panel's kind (the sensible default);
    removing the chip searches every kind, grouped by kind. Sibling of the wrap for the same reason
    the toast is: `layout()` hides the wrap the moment the stack empties. */}
<div class="pk-pop" data-ph-addpop data-ph-pop hidden>
  <div class="pk-pop-head"><span data-ph-addhead>Open another panel beside</span></div>
  <label class="input input-sm pk-pop-search">
    <span class="iconify lucide--search size-3.5 opacity-50" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <input type="search" data-ph-addsearch placeholder="Search…" aria-label="Search what you can open" autocomplete="off" />
  </label>
  <div class="pk-pop-list" data-ph-addlist></div>
</div>

<div class="ph-undo" data-ph-undo hidden role="status">
  <span class="iconify ph-undo-ic size-4" data-ph-undo-ic aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="ph-undo-label" data-ph-undo-label></span>
  <button type="button" class="ph-undo-act" data-ph-undo-act hidden></button>
  <span class="ph-undo-bar" data-ph-undo-bar aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>

<script>
  import { createPanelStack } from './panelStack';
  import { kindFor, kindOfPanel, onKindsSettled, setPanelHost, type HostPanel } from './panelHostRegistry';
  import { createPanelAddPicker } from './panelAddPicker';
  import { isSplit, setSplit, applySplit } from './splitView';
  import { MOBILE_MEDIA } from '../../lib/mobileNav';
  import { refreshTooltip } from './tooltip';
  import { pushEscape } from '../../lib/escapeStack';

  function wirePanelHost() {
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-panelhost]');
    if (!root || root.dataset.phWired) return;
    root.dataset.phWired = '1';
    const panelsEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-panels]')!;
    const railEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-rail]')!;
    const railAdd = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-add]')!;
    const foldIc = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-fold-ic]')!;
    const collapseAllBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-collapse-all]')!;
    const closeAllBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-close-all]')!;
    const splitBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-split]')!;
    const splitDiv = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-split-div]')!;

    // ── Split view (components/ui/splitView.ts) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    // The mode lives here because the stack is the thing it changes: the content floor and the page
    // inset are both read off this one boolean. The sidebar collapse and the persisted flag are the
    // module's; the arithmetic is the stack's.
    //
    // The flag is keyed by SECTION — a Data table and a report document are not the same trade, so
    // each page gets its own answer. The argument is in `splitView.ts`.
    const sectionName = root.dataset.phSection || 'data';
    /**
     * ── SPLIT IS A WIDE-ONLY MODE (Oleh, 2026-08-11) ────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * *«в нас буде завжди панель запінена на мобільному вью. Нема сенсу, тут неможливо відкрити дві
     * паралельних. Тобто і кнопка split, і overlay має зникнути.»*
     *
     * He is describing something the stack already half-knew. Below the boundary `layout()` shows
     * exactly ONE panel — every other is `display: none` — and `openBeside` drills in place instead
     * of spawning. So split view at 400px was a toggle that reserved page width for panels that
     * could never sit beside anything: it moved the content, changed nothing visible, and offered a
     * mode as a choice when only one of its two states was reachable. A control that cannot do what
     * it says is worse than no control.
     *
     * The PREFERENCE is not touched. `isSplit()` still reads what the user chose for this section;
     * this only refuses to APPLY it while the window is too narrow to honour it, so a laptop that
     * gets dragged narrow and back finds its layout where it left it. That is the same reasoning as
     * rule 2 in `splitView.ts` — a layout mode must not undo a choice it did not make.
     */
    const narrowMq = window.matchMedia(MOBILE_MEDIA);
    let split = isSplit(sectionName) && !narrowMq.matches;
    const workEl = () => document.getElementById('layout-work');
    /**
     * `aria-pressed` = the state you are IN. The tooltip = the state the press moves you TO. They
     * are deliberately opposite, because a toggle's hint is a promise about the click, not a
     * readout — and the readout already exists, twice, in `aria-pressed` and in the lit control.
     *
     * `refreshTooltip` is what stops the bubble lying. The click that flips this also focuses the
     * button, and `focusin` re-shows the tooltip with the OLD text BEFORE this runs, so without the
     * repaint the hint sat under the pointer describing the mode you had just left.
     */
    function paintSplit() {
      // `hidden`, not a CSS rule: the control must leave the TAB ORDER too, and a button you can
      // still reach with the keyboard to turn on a mode the layout will refuse is the same defect
      // in a quieter form. The hairline above it goes with it — it exists to separate ＋ from the
      // group below, and with the group gone it would be a rule under a single button.
      const avail = !narrowMq.matches;
      splitBtn.hidden = !avail;
      splitDiv.hidden = !avail;
      /* ＋ IS THE ONE CONTROL THAT SURVIVES NARROW, so it is the one whose copy must survive with
         it. Below the boundary it no longer opens anything BESIDE anything — `mobileOpen` replaces
         — and a label reading "Open another panel beside" beside a stack that cannot hold two is
         the same kind of lie the four hidden controls were, just written down instead of drawn.
         Repainted from here because this function already owns every string the boundary changes,
         and `refreshTooltip` for the same reason `paintSplit` needs it: the bubble can be on screen
         when the window crosses. */
      const addCopy = avail ? 'Open another panel beside' : 'Open something else here';
      railAdd.setAttribute('aria-label', addCopy);
      railAdd.setAttribute('data-tip', addCopy);
      refreshTooltip(railAdd);
      // The picker's own heading is the same sentence on a bigger surface — it opens FROM this
      // button and must not contradict it.
      const addHead = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-addhead]');
      if (addHead) addHead.textContent = addCopy;
      if (!avail) return;
      splitBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', split ? 'true' : 'false');
      const tip = split
        ? 'Turn off split view — panels go back to covering the page'
        : 'Turn on split view — panels sit beside the page';
      splitBtn.setAttribute('data-tip', tip);
      splitBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', split ? 'Turn off split view' : 'Turn on split view');
      refreshTooltip(splitBtn);
    }
    /** The page inset = the measured stack (control column included), or 0 when it is not showing.
     *  The emptiness test is the PANEL COUNT, not `root.hidden`: `teardown()` fires `onEmpty` before
     *  it hides the root, so a `hidden` test left a 40px column-shaped hole in the page after
     *  "Close all" (caught in the browser — both gates were green).
     *
     *  MEASURE THE OCCUPANTS, NEVER THE ROW. This read used to be `panelsEl.getBoundingClientRect()
     *  .width`, which was only ever right by accident: `.ph-panels` was shrink-to-fit, so its box
     *  and its contents were the same number. `2cc56b4` gave that box `left: 0` for a real reason
     *  (the sheets' `calc(100% - 40px)` was resolving against a parent their own width had sized —
     *  four hosts, four left edges, 77.80px of spread at 390), and the same edit silently turned
     *  this measurement into the VIEWPORT. Split then set `padding-right: 1728px` on a 1728px page:
     *  the work column's content box went to zero and the whole page painted as one vertical strip
     *  (reproduced on /data and /schema, 2026-08-18). A width fix and a width read shared one number
     *  and nothing connected them — no gate models a CSS change arriving in a JS measurement, and
     *  all four were green.
     *
     *  So take the distance from the row's right edge to the LEFTMOST painted child. The stack is
     *  right-anchored (`justify-content: flex-end`) with the control column as the first flex child,
     *  so that span is exactly rail + sheets, and it stays correct however many panels are open and
     *  whatever `left` the row is given next. `width > 0` skips the closing panel's corpse, which
     *  `closePanel` marks `hidden` on the same frame precisely so this read cannot count it. */
    function syncSplitWidth(open: number) {
      let px = 0;
      if (split && open) {
        const right = panelsEl.getBoundingClientRect().right;
        let left = right;
        for (const child of Array.from(panelsEl.children)) {
          const r = child.getBoundingClientRect();
          if (r.width > 0) left = Math.min(left, r.left);
        }
        px = Math.max(0, Math.round(right - left));
      }
      document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--ph-split-w', px + 'px');
    }
    // Restore on load: re-rails the sidebar, stamps the html attribute, locks the sidebar control.
    // Called on the OFF path too — otherwise a section where split is off inherits the `data-split-
    // view='on'` attribute and the LOCKED sidebar toggle from the section the user came from, since
    // Astro's ClientRouter carries the root element's attributes across a soft swap.
    applySplit(split, false);
    paintSplit();

    /**
     * Crossing the boundary in a live window. Dragging a window past 1280 is the ordinary case here,
     * not an edge one — this UI is built to live in a panel the user RESIZES.
     *
     * Narrowing must drop the mode without touching the stored preference; widening must put back
     * exactly what that preference said, which is why the flag is re-read from `isSplit()` rather
     * than remembered in a variable. Everything else — the page inset, the sidebar, the html
     * attribute — is already downstream of `applySplit` + `layout`, so this only has to state the
     * boolean and let the two functions that own the consequences run.
     *
     * `applySplit`, NOT `setSplit`, and that distinction is the whole reason the two were separated:
     * `setSplit` persists, so narrowing through it overwrote the section's stored answer with `'0'`
     * and a window dragged narrow and back came up split-off. Measured, not reasoned about.
     */
    narrowMq.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
      const want = e.matches ? false : isSplit(sectionName);
      if (want !== split) {
        split = want;
        applySplit(split, false);
        if (!split) stack.unparkAuto();
      }
      paintSplit();
      if (!root.hidden) stack.layout();
    });

    // ── The one toast (pattern-undo-toast) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // ONE duration drives both the countdown bar and the dismiss timer, so the bar can never lie
    // about how long the offer stands.
    const UNDO_MS = 4000;
    let undoTimer = 0;
    const toastEl = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-undo]')!;
    function restartCountdown(el: HTMLElement, ms: number) {
      el.style.setProperty('--ph-undo-ms', ms + 'ms');
      el.classList.remove('ph-undo-run'); void el.offsetWidth; el.classList.add('ph-undo-run');
    }
    function showToast(label: string, action: { label: string; run: () => void; icon?: string } | null, ms = UNDO_MS) {
      const lbl = toastEl.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-undo-label]'); if (lbl) lbl.textContent = label;
      const btn = toastEl.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-ph-undo-act]');
      if (btn) { btn.hidden = !action; if (action) { btn.textContent = action.label; btn.onclick = () => { action.run(); hideToast(); }; } else btn.onclick = null; }
      toastEl.classList.toggle('ph-undo-actionable', !!action);
      const ic = toastEl.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-undo-ic]');
      // `icon` names the ACTION, not the fact that one exists: rotate-ccw only ever means "Undo".
      if (ic) ic.className = `iconify ph-undo-ic ${action ? (action.icon || 'lucide--rotate-ccw') : 'lucide--info'} size-4`;
      toastEl.hidden = false; restartCountdown(toastEl, ms);
      window.clearTimeout(undoTimer); undoTimer = window.setTimeout(hideToast, ms);
    }
    function hideToast() { window.clearTimeout(undoTimer); toastEl.classList.remove('ph-undo-run'); toastEl.hidden = true; }

    // The ONE picker, built after the stack (it asks the stack which kind to scope to). Declared
    // here because the stack config below already needs a way to shut it.
    let addPicker: ReturnType<typeof createPanelAddPicker> | null = null;
    const closePicker = () => addPicker?.close();

    // The Pin toast names WHAT was pinned, and a run, a record, a file and an entity are pinned for
    // different reasons. `createPanelStack` reads `text.detached` at the moment it fires, so a getter
    // over the panel being detached says the right sentence without widening the controller's API.
    let detaching: HostPanel | null = null;

    // ── The ONE stack. Every host hook below is a dispatch through the kind registry. ──────────
    const stack = createPanelStack<HostPanel>({
      root, panelsEl, railEl,
      countEl: root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-count]'),
      shutterEl: root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-shutter]'),
      // The control column's whole background takes the same stack-resize drag as the pill.
      // panelStack guards it on the event TARGET, so a pointerdown that started on a control never
      // begins a drag.
      resizeEl: railEl,
      // The peek is per-PAGE (64 on Data, 150 on Schema) — see the file header. Do not unify, and
      // do not restate it here: it arrives as a prop so the number lives in one place per page.
      DEFW: 480, MINW: 360, TABLEGAP: Number(root.dataset.phTablegap) || 64,
      // The content floor in split is per-PAGE for the same reason TABLEGAP is — see the file header
      // for the three numbers and what each was measured against. Stated as CONTENT: the stack
      // measures the 40px control column itself and adds it, so this never drifts from the CSS.
      MINCONTENT: Number(root.dataset.phMincontent) || 700,
      sessionKey: `panels:${sectionName}`,
      isSplit: () => split,
      // Budget against the WORK COLUMN, never the viewport: `window.innerWidth` overcounted by the
      // full 256px sidebar. Harmless while the stack overlays, fatal in split.
      containerW: () => workEl()?.getBoundingClientRect().width || window.innerWidth,
      // The floor is a question asked of the PANEL, because this stack holds four kinds of sheet.
      minwOf: (p) => kindOfPanel(p)?.minw ?? 360,
      // ONE remembered width app-wide (catalog `pattern-side-panel-width`).
      widthVar: '--panel-w',
      cls: { collapsed: 'ps-collapsed', focus: 'ps-focus', anchor: 'ps-anchor', flash: 'ps-flash', atLimit: 'ps-atlimit', gripOn: 'ps-grip-on', dragging: 'ps-dragging', reordering: 'ps-reordering' },
      // NEUTRAL hooks: one host can only pass ONE selector, and it has to match every kind it holds.
      // Every sheet template stamps `data-ps-panel` / `data-ps-grip` / `data-ps-reorder` beside its
      // own `data-{prefix}-*` hooks (those stay — other code and CSS use them).
      reorderSel: '[data-ps-reorder]', panelSel: '[data-ps-panel]', gripSel: '[data-ps-grip]',
      // `buildPanel` gets the KIND-PREFIXED id — the only thing available at clone time that says
      // WHICH template this panel is and which controller to instantiate.
      buildPanel: (id) => {
        const k = kindFor(id)!;
        const p = k.buildPanel(id);
        p.kind = k.prefix;
        return p;
      },
      loadInto: (p, id) => {
        const k = kindOfPanel(p);
        // A KIND cannot be reloaded into the wrong SHEET. Every route that crosses kinds builds a
        // new panel; bail rather than leave a panel holding a stale body.
        if (!k || !id.startsWith(k.prefix)) return;
        k.loadInto(p, id);
      },
      render: (p) => kindOfPanel(p)?.render(p),
      panelKey: (p) => kindOfPanel(p)?.panelKey(p) || '',
      canOpen: (id) => { const k = kindFor(id); return !!k && (k.canOpen ? k.canOpen(id) : true); },
      onMissing: (id) => { root.hidden = false; kindFor(id)?.onMissing?.(id); },
      /**
       * ── NARROW IS ONE PANEL, AND MEANS IT (Oleh, 2026-08-12) ────────────────────────────────
       *
       * *«зараз, коли користувач відкриває цю панель, в нього є змога відкрити ще одну всередині,
       * яка перекриває наявну… накладаються панель на панель, і вся ця механіка забагована»*.
       *
       * This branch was the way in. A kind that can drill in place does; a kind that CANNOT — the
       * five sheets are separate components, so an `ask:` id has no business inside an `entity:`
       * sheet — used to `createPanel`, which is the one line in the narrow path that made the stack
       * two panels deep. `layout()` then hid all but the focused one, so the second panel was
       * invisible and fully alive: it kept its place in the stack, it took the focus, and Escape or
       * Close revealed a panel underneath that the user had no way to know was still there.
       *
       * A hidden panel is worse than a visible one. It is the same object the rail controls used to
       * promise and could not deliver, except this one the user could actually create.
       *
       * So a cross-kind open REPLACES. `closeAll()` is the silent teardown on purpose — it is the
       * one written for "the context did this, not the user", which is exactly the case: the ask
       * was "open X", and closing Y is the consequence of a rule, not an act to offer an Undo for.
       * `open()` afterwards is the stack's own single-panel verb; it creates the anchor because the
       * stack is now empty, and takes the right sheet from `kindFor(id)`.
       *
       * WHAT THIS COSTS, stated: the replaced panel does not come back with Back. Back is the
       * sheet's own in-panel history and cannot cross a component boundary. Wide is untouched —
       * this whole function only runs below the boundary.
       */
      mobileOpen: (source, id) => {
        const k = kindFor(id); if (!k) return;
        if (source && source.kind === k.prefix && k.mobileOpen) { k.mobileOpen(source, id); return; }
        const ex = stack.panels().find((q) => kindOfPanel(q)?.panelKey(q) === id);
        if (ex) { stack.setFocus(ex); stack.layout(); return; }
        stack.closeAll();
        stack.open(id);
      },
      onFocus: (p, active) => kindOfPanel(p)?.onFocus?.(p, active),
      onDestroy: (p) => kindOfPanel(p)?.onDestroy?.(p),
      toast: showToast, hideToast,
      text: {
        closed: 'Panel closed',
        capReached: (m) => `Can't add another panel — maximum of ${m} reached.`,
        evicted: (m) => `Closed oldest panel — ${m} max`,
        evictedForRoom: (n) => n === 1
          ? 'Closed the oldest panel — the list keeps its minimum width.'
          : `Closed ${n} panels — the list keeps its minimum width.`,
        get detached() {
          return (detaching && kindOfPanel(detaching)?.detachedText)
            || 'Pinned — this panel is yours now. The next thing you open starts a fresh anchor beside the list.';
        },
        closedAll: (n) => `Closed ${n} ${n === 1 ? 'panel' : 'panels'}`,
      },
      // The two bulk verbs name what they act on — the count is what makes a bulk verb safe to
      // press — and they only exist once there are enough panels for "all" to mean anything.
      onLayout: () => {
        const n = stack.panels().length;
        // No count threshold. These two are invisible at rest and revealed on hover, so hiding
        // them below N buys nothing visually — at rest the column looks identical either way — and
        // it costs the one thing that matters: the control vanishes exactly when the pointer
        // arrives for it. A set of controls that changes shape as you add panels is the defect,
        // not the chrome. They are simply always available, and always name their count.
        const what = `all ${n} ${n === 1 ? 'panel' : 'panels'}`;
        // ONE control, two directions. Folding everything is how you get the table back; the way
        // OUT of that was dragging the shutter open, which is a gesture, not a verb. The glyph turns
        // with the meaning: arrows toward each other fold, arrows apart unfold.
        const folded = stack.allCollapsed();
        foldIc.className = `iconify ${folded ? 'lucide--chevrons-left-right' : 'lucide--chevrons-right-left'} size-4`;
        collapseAllBtn.setAttribute('data-tip', folded ? `Expand ${what}` : `Collapse ${what} to strips`);
        collapseAllBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', folded ? `Expand ${what}` : `Collapse ${what}`);
        closeAllBtn.setAttribute('data-tip', `Close ${what}`);
        closeAllBtn.setAttribute('aria-label', `Close ${what}`);
        syncSplitWidth(n);
      },
      flash: (el) => { el.classList.remove('ps-flash'); void el.offsetWidth; el.classList.add('ps-flash'); },
      // `teardown()` hides the root without a `layout()`, so the page inset has to be released here
      // as well — otherwise closing the last panel leaves a column-shaped hole in the page.
      onEmpty: () => { closePicker(); syncSplitWidth(0); },
    });

    // ── The API the kinds call back through ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    /**
     * A plain open lands in the ANCHOR — but only if the anchor can HOLD it. With one kind that was
     * always true; with four it is not: the anchor on the Attachments tab is a file sheet, and a
     * `record:` id loaded into it would silently no-op (`loadInto` refuses a foreign kind) leaving
     * the user staring at the file they just clicked away from. A cross-kind plain open therefore
     * opens BESIDE, which is also what the surface wants — the crumb describes the anchor, so
     * replacing the anchor would throw away the thing the crumb is about.
     * Caught in the browser, not by a gate: both linters were green with the click doing nothing.
     */
    function hostOpen(id: string, init?: (p: HostPanel) => void) {
      const a = stack.anchorOf();
      // `init` travels down BOTH branches. It used to be dropped here, so a record asked for at a
      // particular comment lost the comment whenever the anchor happened to be another kind.
      if (a && a.kind !== kindFor(id)?.prefix) { stack.openBeside(stack.focused(), id, init); return; }
      stack.open(id, init);
    }
    setPanelHost({
      open: (id, init) => hostOpen(id, init),
      openBeside: (s, id, init) => stack.openBeside(s, id, init),
      createPanel: (id, anchor, init) => stack.createPanel(id, anchor, init),
      closePanel: (p) => stack.closePanel(p),
      evictLRU: (a) => stack.evictLRU(a),
      panels: () => stack.panels(),
      focused: () => stack.focused(),
      atCap: () => stack.atCap(),
      layout: () => stack.layout(),
      toast: showToast,
      mounted: () => true,
    });

    // ── The stack verbs that live in every panel's header ─────────────────────────────────────
    // Close / Pin / Collapse are STACK-level, not content-level, so the host answers them for every
    // kind through the neutral hooks — rather than four copies of the same three branches.
    // (The entity sheet's × stays with `makeEntityPanel`: it commits an open description edit first,
    // then calls back into `closePanel` through its own `onClose`.)
    panelsEl.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const target = e.target as HTMLElement;
      const sheet = target.closest<HTMLElement>('[data-ps-panel]');
      if (!sheet) return;
      const p = stack.panels().find((q) => q.el === sheet);
      if (!p) return;
      // A parked strip: click anywhere but its ✕ expands it back (auto-accordion / collapse-rail).
      // stopPropagation so the owning kind's own delegate does not also read the click as content.
      if (p.collapsed && !target.closest('[data-ps-close], [data-ep-close]')) {
        e.stopPropagation(); stack.expandPanel(p.uid); return;
      }
      if (stack.focused() !== p) stack.setFocus(p);
      if (target.closest('[data-ps-close]')) { e.stopPropagation(); stack.closePanel(p); return; }
      if (target.closest('[data-ps-detach]')) { e.stopPropagation(); detaching = p; stack.detachPanel(p); detaching = null; return; }
      if (target.closest('[data-ps-park]')) { e.stopPropagation(); stack.parkPanel(p.uid); return; }
      if (target.closest('[data-ps-reorder]')) { e.stopPropagation(); return; } // the pointerdown drag owns it
    });

    // ── The control column ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // ＋ opens ONE picker over every kind registered on this page (components/ui/panelAddPicker.ts),
    // scoped by default to the ACTIVE TAB's source where that tab has one, else to the kind of the
    // panel you are in — clearing the scope searches everything. It replaced a per-kind popover,
    // which is why the ＋ could only ever add what you were already looking at.
    addPicker = createPanelAddPicker({
      pop: document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-addpop]')!,
      input: document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-ph-addsearch]')!,
      list: document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-ph-addlist]')!,
      // The anchor is the fallback for a focused kind that offers nothing to search.
      focusedKind: () => stack.focused()?.kind || stack.anchorOf()?.kind || null,
      // First half of the picker's `TAB_SOURCE` key — the same section name split view is keyed by.
      section: sectionName,
    });
    railEl.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const t = e.target as HTMLElement;
      if (t.closest('[data-ph-add]')) {
        e.stopPropagation();
        // Toggle: a second press on the ＋ closes what it opened. The picker's own visibility is
        // the only record of that — a `data-ph-open` flag beside it was the same fact written twice.
        if (addPicker!.isOpen()) { addPicker!.close(); return; }
        addPicker!.open(railAdd.getBoundingClientRect());
        return;
      }
      // Both bulk verbs close the picker first — it is anchored to a column that is about to change
      // under it. Collapse all parks every panel and keeps its state; Close all tears the stack down
      // THROUGH the undo toast, which restores all of them in order.
      // The mode toggle. Switching ON collapses the left navigation ONCE, here — never on panel
      // open (reflow under a clicking hand is the defect `decision-panels-overlay-not-reflow`
      // exists to prevent). Switching OFF leaves the sidebar exactly where the user left it.
      if (t.closest('[data-ph-split]')) {
        e.stopPropagation(); closePicker();
        split = !split; setSplit(sectionName, split); paintSplit();
        // Switching split OFF hands the width back, so it must hand the PANELS back too. `collapsed`
        // is stored on the panel and `layout()` only ever parks, so without this the strips the mode
        // made stayed strips in a width that could hold them — the toggle looked like it ate panels.
        // Only what the ACCORDION parked comes back (`autoParked`); a panel the user parked by hand,
        // or via Collapse all, stays parked. `unparkAuto` re-lays-out, so anything that still does not
        // fit is parked again on the same frame and the floor is never bent.
        if (!split) stack.unparkAuto();
        // The sidebar's own 300ms transition means the work column is still moving; re-layout after
        // it settles so the budget is taken from the width the page actually ends up with.
        stack.layout();
        window.setTimeout(() => {
          stack.layout();
          // Switching split ON is an explicit act, so it may cost panels: ten strips at a 1440 laptop
          // would leave 427px of listing, well under the floor. Resizing the window never does this.
          //
          // AFTER the sidebar has finished railing, and that is the whole point of it living in here.
          // Enabling split widens the work column by the sidebar's 192px, but the rail animates over
          // 300ms — so an eviction fired on the click read the OLD, narrower width and closed panels
          // that were about to fit. Measured on /schema at 1440, 2026-08-06: five panels, space for
          // five once railed, and four were closed. Everything in between is `layout()`, which only
          // PARKS — non-destructive and reversible — so the delay costs nothing and buys the budget
          // being taken from the width the page actually ends up with.
          if (split) {
            const closed = stack.evictForRoom();
            // `evictLRU` removes the element but does NOT re-lay-out — the stack's other eviction
            // caller happens to `layout()` right after for its own reasons, so nothing ever noticed.
            // In split it is load-bearing: `--ph-split-w` is published from `onLayout`, so without
            // this the page inset kept paying for the panels that were just closed. Measured on
            // /schema at 1440: two evicted, inset 630 against a real stack of 538, painting a 92px
            // seam and holding the content 54px under its own floor.
            if (closed) stack.layout();
            stack.announceEviction(closed);
          }
        }, 320);
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-ph-collapse-all]')) { closePicker(); if (stack.allCollapsed()) stack.expandAll(); else stack.collapseAll(); return; }
      if (t.closest('[data-ph-close-all]')) { closePicker(); stack.closeAllWithUndo(); return; }
    });
    // Any click outside the column dismisses the picker; the popover swallows its own.
    document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if (!addPicker!.isOpen()) return;
      if (railEl.contains(e.target as Node)) return;
      if ((e.target as HTMLElement).closest('[data-ph-pop]')) return;
      closePicker();
    });

    // ── Escape ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // ONE handler for the whole page. It asks the focused panel's KIND first — an open description
    // editor, an in-place field view or a controller with its own Escape consumes the key — and
    // closes the panel only when the kind declines. This replaces the two cross-stack hacks that
    // used to guess at each other's DOM: DataChangelog peeking at `[data-recordpanel]:not([hidden])`
    // (which after the merge would be present-but-empty, making Esc a silent no-op) and RecordPanel
    // stopPropagation-ing the document `[data-entity-open]` delegate.
    // It is an `escapeStack` entry, so "nothing open here" is answered with `false` and the press
    // falls through to whatever is underneath instead of being spent on a hidden host.
    pushEscape({
      label: 'PanelHost',
      onEscape: () => {
        if (root.hidden) return false;
        const p = stack.focused() || stack.panels()[stack.panels().length - 1];
        if (!p) return false;
        if (kindOfPanel(p)?.onEsc?.(p)) return true;
        stack.closePanel(p);
        return true;
      },
    });

    // Leaving the tab abandons every panel's context — ONE listener now, so the stack closes once.
    // Tabs inside a section no longer cost you your panels. They used to: this listener called
    // `closeAll()`, so five minutes of arranging a view died the moment you looked at another tab
    // (Dan + Oleh, Slack 2026-08-05). Dan believed Schema already kept them and Data did not —
    // measured, and NEITHER did: both hosts carried this same line, so the two sections were equally
    // lossy and Schema only hid it better. A tab is a change of what you are LOOKING at, not of what
    // you are working with; the section boundary is the one that resets, and that is a real page load,
    // so it needs no code. The picker still closes — it is scoped to the tab it was opened from.
    // Re-layout because the tab's content can change the work column's width.
    document.addEventListener('app:tabchange', () => { closePicker(); stack.layout(); });
    window.addEventListener('resize', () => { if (!root.hidden) stack.layout(); });
    // Restoring must wait for the KINDS, not for a clock. Every panel kind registers itself from its
    // own module script, and module execution order across a page is not this file's to decide —
    // `canOpen` answers "no" to every saved id until the kind that owns it exists. This used to be a
    // `setTimeout(…, 0)` on the reasoning that one macrotask lands after every module. In dev Astro
    // serves each component script as its OWN `<script type="module">`, so it does not: measured on
    // `/schema`, the timer fired before `entity:` registered and both saved panels were dropped in
    // silence. `onKindsSettled` fires after the last registration instead, whatever the ordering.
    //     // Unsubscribe once the arrangement is fully back, and not before: a kind can register after the
    // first burst settles (dev serves each component script as its own module), and dropping the
    // subscription early would strand its panels. Holding it forever is the other failure — every
    // later burst would run this closure over a torn-down stack — hence the explicit `off()`.
    const offRestore = onKindsSettled(() => { if (stack.restoreSession() === 0) offRestore(); });
    // …and unsubscribe unconditionally when the page goes away. A saved id whose kind never registers
    // on THIS page keeps `pending` above zero forever — real on /data, where the host mounts on
    // `hasData` while the entity and media kinds have guards of their own — so without this the
    // callback outlives its stack and every later registration burst runs it over detached DOM.
    document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', () => offRestore(), { once: true });
  }
  wirePanelHost();
  document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', wirePanelHost);
</script>

<style is:global>
  /* Global + a unique `.ph-` prefix: panel bodies are rendered via innerHTML, which scoped styles
     never reach. The column, the shutter and the sheet STATE classes are the shared `ps-*` set in
     styles/global.css — what is left here is only this host's own frame and its toast. */
  .ph-wrap { position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 62; pointer-events: none; }
  .ph-wrap[hidden] { display: none; }
  /* Coplanar right-anchored stack (array[0] = anchor = leftmost); the control column is the first
     flex child, so it sits at the outer edge of the leftmost sheet.

     `left: 0` IS LOAD-BEARING (audit X-M3, D45). Without it this box had top/right/bottom and no
     horizontal size, so it was SHRINK-TO-FIT — its width came from the sheets inside it. Those
     sheets are the narrow panel, `width: calc(100% - 40px)` (16-responsive.md §8), and `100%` of a
     shrink-to-fit parent means "100% of what I myself just made this parent", which is circular: the
     sheet painted 288.79px at x=101.21 on /schema at 390 AND, identically, at 425 — viewport-
     independent, against the certified 350 at x=40. With a second panel open the row exceeded
     `max-width: 100vw`, the box clamped to the viewport, and the certified number appeared — which
     is exactly why the same declaration measured correct on /data (two panels in the census sample)
     and wrong on /schema. Four hosts, four widths, four left edges, 77.80px of spread at 390.
     `left: 0` + `right: 0` makes the containing block the viewport, so `calc(100% - 40px)` resolves
     against the viewport whatever is inside. `max-width: 100vw` is now redundant and kept only as
     the belt on a box the sheets can no longer size.

     THE POINTER-EVENTS SWAP IS PART OF THE SAME FIX, not tidying. This stack is NON-MODAL — no
     scrim, the page stays clickable behind it (see the comment on `.ph-panels` in the markup). While
     the box hugged its content, `pointer-events: auto` on it was harmless. Full-width, that same
     declaration would make an invisible box swallow every click across the viewport, so the `auto`
     moves down to the children that are actually painted and the wrapper goes back to `none` like
     `.ph-wrap` above it. `> *` and not a class list: the sheets are appended here at runtime by four
     different hosts, so a selector naming any of them would miss the next one. */
  .ph-panels { position: absolute; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: row; justify-content: flex-end; max-width: 100vw; pointer-events: none; }
  .ph-panels > * { pointer-events: auto; }

  /* FIXED, not absolute. It reads as a detail and is the whole bug: this toast used to live
     inside `.ph-wrap` (`position: fixed; inset: 0`), which is what made `bottom: 24px` mean
     the bottom of the SCREEN. Moving it out of the wrapper — needed, because `layout()` hides
     the wrapper the moment the stack empties and the toast measured 0x0 — quietly re-based it
     on the page, so it rendered at top 2945 of a 3000px document: fired, correct, unreadable
     unless you scrolled the listing to its end (measured 2026-08-05). Anchoring it to the
     viewport itself makes it independent of whatever ancestor it happens to sit under. */  .ph-undo { position: fixed; left: 50%; bottom: 24px; transform: translateX(-50%); z-index: 63; pointer-events: auto; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; max-width: min(92vw, 30rem); padding: 12px 16px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--color-neutral); color: var(--color-neutral-content); border-radius: var(--r-12); box-shadow: 0 8px 24px oklch(0 0 0 / .28); font-size: var(--t-13); } /* ds-ok: translateX(-50%) centering, not spacing */
  .ph-undo[hidden] { display: none; }
  .ph-undo-label { min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
  .ph-undo-act { flex: none; margin-left: 4px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-primary); background: none; border: 0; cursor: pointer; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: var(--r-6); font-size: var(--t-13); }
  .ph-undo-act[hidden] { display: none; }
  .ph-undo-act:hover { background: oklch(from var(--color-primary) l c h / .16); }
  .ph-undo-bar { position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; height: 2px; background: var(--color-primary); transform-origin: left center; transform: scaleX(0); }
  .ph-undo:not(.ph-undo-actionable) .ph-undo-bar { display: none; }
  .ph-undo.ph-undo-run.ph-undo-actionable .ph-undo-bar { animation: ph-undo-count var(--ph-undo-ms, 4000ms) linear forwards; }
  @keyframes ph-undo-count { from { transform: scaleX(1); } to { transform: scaleX(0); } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .ph-undo.ph-undo-run .ph-undo-bar { animation: none; transform: scaleX(1); } }
</style>
